/// `wa:stt` — the speech queue's wire layer, as a component (`docs/QUEUE.md` phase 2,
/// `docs/CORE-SPLIT.md`).
///
/// The queue has two halves and they fail in different ways. **What a call means** — is this envelope
/// the protocol's, is the caller one of the owner's nodes, is it inside the skew window, was this
/// `rid` already answered, what is the body, what bytes go back — is a rule over bytes and a set of
/// seen ids: no socket, no clock of its own, no key, no queue. **What it touches** — the `stt.requests`
/// queue over the node's kv, the audio store, the worker's own recogniser, the relay and the node's
/// private key — is the node's, and a component must not own any of it.
///
/// This package is the first half, and it is deliberately small: the six operations the five
/// `host.stt_*` calls apply before they reach the queue. Nothing here is a JSON document except where
/// the value *is* the wire: `body-json` is the envelope's own body (opaque to this layer, as the
/// protocol says), and the two reply builders take the body the node composed. Everything else is a
/// record or a case of `refusal`, so a refusal never reads as a string the caller has to parse.
package wa:stt@0.1.0;

interface types {
    /// The version this build speaks (`protocol::VERSION`); an envelope carrying another is refused
    /// with it, not guessed at.
    record envelope {
        /// `take`, `audio`, `beat`, `seg`, `done` or `fail` — the op as the wire spells it.
        op: string,
        from-node: string,
        to: string,
        ts-ms: s64,
        rid: string,
        /// The op's own body, exactly as it crossed the wire.
        body-json: string,
    }

    /// Every reason a call, an envelope or a reply is refused — the vocabulary
    /// `wa-stt-remote::protocol::Refusal` has, so both paths print the same words for the same fault.
    /// `failed` and `malformed-unprocessable` carry the producer's own short reason, which is
    /// sanitized before it is ever rendered.
    variant refusal {
        /// A protocol generation this node does not speak.
        version(u64),
        /// `to` is not this node.
        wrong-target,
        /// The caller is not one of the owner's nodes.
        unknown-caller,
        /// The call's timestamp is outside the skew window.
        stale,
        /// The signature does not cover this call.
        bad-signature,
        /// This `rid` was already answered.
        replayed,
        /// The envelope or a body field is not what the protocol says, carrying the field's name.
        malformed(string),
        /// The caller does not hold this job's lease (it expired, or another worker has it).
        lease-lost,
        /// No such job or handle.
        not-found,
        /// The job went stale: it is not decoded and its result is not posted.
        stale-job,
        /// The audio is larger than the producer will move over the relay.
        too-large,
        /// The caller asked before it may (the local worker's grace has not elapsed).
        not-yet,
        /// The producing node's own store failed, carrying the sanitized reason. Never a success.
        failed(string),
    }

    /// What one call turned into: the admitted envelope, or the reply bytes the wire carries.
    ///
    /// A refusal is rendered HERE rather than handed back as a case, because the bytes are what the
    /// peer reads and this is the layer that owns them: `refused` is exactly the reply the native
    /// path answers for the same fault, produced from the same `Refusal`, and the host hands it on
    /// without composing a word of its own.
    variant admitted {
        call(envelope),
        /// A refusal: its own word (`Refusal::as_str()` — `unknown_caller`, `replayed_request`, …,
        /// which is what the node's counters are keyed by) and the reply bytes the peer reads.
        refused(refusal-word),
    }

    /// One refusal as the wire and the counters spell it. The word is produced by the crate the node
    /// also links, so the host never reconstructs a case to count it and never invents a spelling.
    record refusal-word {
        word: string,
        bytes: list<u8>,
    }
}

/// Every waiting call here is an `async func`: the guest's task suspends and the runtime keeps
/// running other tasks, short calls included (the async rule, Victor 26/11). `sanitize` is the one
/// export that is not, and says why under its own name.
///
/// **Cancellation and backpressure, declared.** A caller cancels an `admit` by dropping the future:
/// this layer starts no task, holds no lock across an await and records nothing for a call that never
/// came back, so dropping one is complete. Backpressure has nothing to declare on this side — every
/// answer is a single document the protocol already bounds (`TooLarge` is a refusal, not a wait) — and
/// this interface deliberately has **no output stream of its own**: the progress a speech job has is
/// the queue's (`wa:queue` `progress`, a `stream<list<u8>>`), and duplicating it here would give the
/// node two streams of one truth.
interface stt {
    use types.{admitted, envelope, refusal};

    /// Decode one call and admit it: the envelope's own fields, the caller the transport verified,
    /// the skew window, and the `rid` ledger this instance holds. The ledger is the seam's state and
    /// it lives here — the host serialises its calls, so one instance is one ledger.
    // async-abi: the caller awaits the answer; the ledger has no progress to stream and no handle to hold.
    admit: async func(me: string, owner-nodes: list<string>, skew-ms: s64, bytes: list<u8>, caller: string, now-ms: s64) -> admitted;

    /// One answer the node composed, as the wire carries it. The node composes the body; the reply's
    /// envelope is the wire's, and that is the half this layer owns.
    // async-abi: one composed body in, the wire bytes out; nothing to stream and nothing to cancel mid-way.
    reply-ok: async func(body-json: string) -> list<u8>;

    /// The one function every rendered refusal and every view line the node shows passes through: control
    /// characters removed, length bounded.
    ///
    /// SYNC ON PURPOSE (the async rule's written reason): a pure map over a string — no I/O, no clock, no
    /// key, nothing to await — called per refusal and per view line, sometimes in a loop. A future around it
    /// would add a suspension point where there is nothing to suspend on and make every call site a task.
    /// It has no output to stream and no backpressure to declare: the bound is the string it is given.
    // sync-export: a pure map over a string - no I/O, no clock, no key, nothing to await (see the note above).
    sanitize: func(text: string) -> string;
}

/// The component: the wire layer this node asks, and nothing to hold but the ids it has answered.
///
/// **What is deliberately NOT here**, so a reader does not look for it: the outbound envelope's
/// composition and its signature message. A producing node does not compose them — the worker inside
/// **wa-stt-remote** does, in the same process as the signer, and `host.sign` is the interpreter's. And
/// the sanitizer every log line passes through is `wa-stt-remote`'s, called where the line is composed;
/// crossing this boundary once per log line would buy nothing and cost a task suspension per line.
world stt-component {
    export stt;
}
